caption.p:
Map‐based cloning of QSn.cau‐7A identifies WAPO‐A1 as the candidate gene for regulating spikelet number per spike (SNS). (A) LOD and additive (Add) effect curves of QSn.cau‐7A across five environments (E1–E5). The LOD threshold is set at 2.5. The horizontal axis indicates genetic position. Environments: E1, Jize, Hebei, China, 2019–2020; E2, Jize, Hebei, 2020–2021; E3, Xinxiang, Henan, 2020–2021; E4, Linfen, Shanxi, 2020–2021; E5, Jize, Hebei, 2022–2023. (B) Fine mapping of QSn.cau‐7A. The top horizontal bar shows the physical positions of 10 Indel markers in the target interval based on the Chinese Spring RefSeq v1.1 genome (IWGSC). Recombinants R297, R333, R354, R425, R426, and R427 from secondary mapping populations were used to narrow the candidate region. Genotypes (left) and corresponding SNS phenotypes (right) are shown: Black, AS420 allele (7A+); white, LX987 allele (7A‐); grey, heterozygous. SNS comparisons between 7A+ and 7A‐ genotypes are presented (right panel; ***p < 0.001; ns, not significant). Annotated high‐confidence genes within the final interval are indicated by arrows; WAPO1‐A (TraesCS7A02G481600) is highlighted in red. Gene structure of WAPO1‐A and sequence variations between AS420 and LX987 in the promoter and coding regions are depicted: Exons (black boxes), introns and flanking regions (black lines), and the F‐box domain (yellow box). Numbers denote positions relative to the start codon. (C) Effect of WAPO1‐A alleles on SNS in the RIL population across environments. Lines carrying the AS420 allele (WAPO

A1b
) or the LX987 allele (WAPO

A1f
) were grouped separately. Box plots show SNS distributions; n indicates the number of lines per group.
